&lt;p&gt;The Human Rights Institute proudly presents our&amp;nbsp;event series,&amp;nbsp;&lt;em&gt;Legal Machinery of Repression&lt;/em&gt;&amp;mdash;a series that brings together voices revealing how repression leaves traces of defiance, memory, and testimony that outlive the machinery itself. Join us for the fifth event in the series focusing&amp;nbsp;on memory, political repression, and the struggle to preserve truth in Venezuela. Memory is never only about the past&amp;mdash;it can also be a way of resisting silence, confronting impunity, and insisting that repression not be forgotten.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This event brings together G&amp;eacute;nesis Mar&amp;iacute;a D&amp;aacute;vila V&amp;aacute;zquez, Venezuelan human rights lawyer and founder of Defiende Venezuela, and Jes&amp;uacute;s Armas, Venezuelan civic leader, former political prisoner, and Obama Foundation Scholar at Columbia University. Drawing from both lived experience and human rights advocacy, the discussion will explore how survivors, families, and civil society actors transform personal and collective trauma into public memory, legal advocacy, and resistance.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Speakers:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;G&amp;eacute;nesis Mar&amp;iacute;a D&amp;aacute;vila V&amp;aacute;zquez:&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;Founder and President, Defiende Venezuela&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
